<section><h2 class="section-title">AIML Essentials Practical – Practical No. 4</h2><h3>EXPERIMENT 4: Evaluation of Classification Model Predictions</h3></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">1. Aim</h2><p>To understand and evaluate the performance of a classification model using a <strong>Confusion Matrix, Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-Score</strong>.</p></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">2. Learning Objectives</h2><p>After completing this practical, students should be able to:</p><ol><li>Understand the relationship between <strong>Actual</strong> and <strong>Predicted</strong> classes.</li><li>Identify <strong>True Positive (TP), True Negative (TN), False Positive (FP), and False Negative (FN)</strong>.</li><li>Construct a <strong>Confusion Matrix</strong>.</li><li>Calculate <strong>Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-Score</strong>.</li><li>Compare the performance of two classification models.</li><li>Select an appropriate evaluation metric based on the application.</li></ol></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">3. Task 1 – Construct a Confusion Matrix</h2><p>A classification model is used to predict whether a student will <strong>Pass (1)</strong> or <strong>Fail (0)</strong>.</p><p>The actual outcomes and model predictions are given below.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Student</th><th>Actual Class</th><th>Predicted Class</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S4</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S5</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S6</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>S7</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>S8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S9</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>S10</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Questions</h3><ol><li>Identify the <strong>True Positive (TP)</strong> cases.</li><li>Identify the <strong>True Negative (TN)</strong> cases.</li><li>Identify the <strong>False Positive (FP)</strong> cases.</li><li>Identify the <strong>False Negative (FN)</strong> cases.</li><li>Calculate the values of TP, TN, FP, and FN.</li><li>Construct the confusion matrix.</li></ol>
<h3>Confusion Matrix Format</h3><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Predicted Positive (1)</th><th>Predicted Negative (0)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Actual Positive (1)</th><td>TP = ?</td><td>FN = ?</td></tr><tr><th>Actual Negative (0)</th><td>FP = ?</td><td>TN = ?</td></tr></tbody></table></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">4. Task 2 – Calculate Classification Evaluation Metrics</h2><p>Using the TP, TN, FP, and FN values obtained in <strong>Task 1</strong>, calculate the following:</p><ol><li><strong>Accuracy</strong></li><li><strong>Precision</strong></li><li><strong>Recall</strong></li><li><strong>F1-Score</strong></li></ol><h3>Formula Reference</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>(TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)</td></tr><tr><td>Precision</td><td>TP / (TP + FP)</td></tr><tr><td>Recall</td><td>TP / (TP + FN)</td></tr><tr><td>F1-Score</td><td>2 × Precision × Recall / (Precision + Recall)</td></tr></tbody></table></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">5. Task 3 – Compare Two Classification Models</h2><p>Two classification models, <strong>Model A</strong> and <strong>Model B</strong>, have been evaluated on the same dataset.</p><h3>Model A</h3><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Predicted Positive</th><th>Predicted Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Actual Positive</th><td>90</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><th>Actual Negative</th><td>10</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Model B</h3><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Predicted Positive</th><th>Predicted Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Actual Positive</th><td>95</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><th>Actual Negative</th><td>25</td><td>75</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Questions</h3><p>For <strong>both Model A and Model B</strong>:</p><ol><li>Identify TP, TN, FP, and FN.</li><li>Calculate Accuracy.</li><li>Calculate Precision.</li><li>Calculate Recall.</li><li>Calculate F1-Score.</li><li>Compare the two models.</li><li>Which model has higher Accuracy?</li><li>Which model has higher Precision?</li><li>Which model has higher Recall?</li><li>Which model has higher F1-Score?</li><li>If both Precision and Recall are important, which model would you prefer? Give a reason.</li></ol></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">6. Important Question</h2><p><strong>Is the model with the highest Accuracy necessarily the best model? Explain your answer.</strong></p></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">7. Task 4 – Choose the Appropriate Evaluation Metric</h2><p>In machine learning, different applications have different consequences for incorrect predictions.</p><p>For each situation:</p><ol><li>Identify the <strong>positive class</strong>.</li><li>Identify whether <strong>False Positive</strong> or <strong>False Negative</strong> is more harmful.</li><li>Select the most appropriate evaluation metric.</li><li>Give a brief justification.</li></ol><h3>Situation A: Disease Detection</h3><p>A machine learning model predicts whether a patient has a serious disease.</p><p>A <strong>False Negative</strong> means that a patient who actually has the disease is predicted as healthy.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> Which metric should receive greater importance: <strong>Precision or Recall</strong>? Explain why.</p><h3>Situation B: Email Spam Detection</h3><p>A machine learning model classifies emails as <strong>Spam</strong> or <strong>Not Spam</strong>.</p><p>A <strong>False Positive</strong> means that a genuine and important email is incorrectly classified as spam.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> Which metric should receive greater importance: <strong>Precision or Recall</strong>? Explain why.</p><h3>Situation C: Fraud Detection</h3><p>A bank uses a machine learning model to identify fraudulent transactions.</p><p>A <strong>False Negative</strong> means that a fraudulent transaction is incorrectly classified as a genuine transaction.</p><ol><li>Which type of error is particularly dangerous?</li><li>Which metric should receive greater importance?</li><li>Why?</li></ol><h3>Situation D: Student Pass/Fail Prediction</h3><p>A college uses a classification model to predict whether students will pass or fail.</p><p>Suppose the consequences of False Positive and False Negative are approximately similar.</p><ol><li>Which evaluation metrics could be considered?</li><li>Would Accuracy alone be sufficient?</li><li>Why might F1-Score also be useful?</li></ol></section><hr>
<section><h2 class="section-title">8. Conclusion</h2><p>Write a short conclusion explaining:</p><p>What you learned about evaluating a classification model and why choosing an appropriate evaluation metric is important.</p></section><hr>
